---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="VPN"
    categorySlug="tools"
    subcategorySlug="vpn"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- What Is a Kill Switch? -->
  <h2 id="what-is-kill-switch">What Is a Kill Switch?</h2>
  <p>
    Picture this. You're <a href="/guides/do-i-need-vpn-cafes/">in a cafe</a>, connected to VPN, sending client files.
    VPN hiccups for half a second.
    Without a kill switch? Your traffic instantly routes through the open network. Real IP exposed.data
    readable. And you probably didn't even notice it happened.
  </p>
  <p>
    A kill switch cuts your internet the moment VPN drops. Nothing in, nothing out. Paranoid? Maybe. But
    I've seen what happens on cafe WiFi.
  </p>

  <!-- How Does It Work? -->
  <h2 id="how-it-works">How It Works</h2>
  <p>Pretty simple actually:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Kill switch watches your VPN connection constantly</li>
    <li>VPN drops (server restart, WiFi hiccup, whatever)</li>
    <li>All traffic blocked. Immediately.</li>
    <li>Stays blocked until VPN reconnects</li>
    <li>Back to normal</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    This happens in milliseconds. Even a half-second drop can't leak data because the kill switch is faster.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Remote Workers Need It -->
  <h2 id="why-you-need-it">Why You Actually Need This</h2>
  <p>VPNs drop more than you'd think:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Server updates (VPN companies do maintenance)</li>
    <li><a href="/tools/how-to-setup-vpn-all-devices/">Switching networks</a> (WiFi to cellular, between access points)
    </li>
    <li>Crappy WiFi (hotels, airports, cafes)</li>
    <li>Random ISP issues</li>
    <li>Software conflicts on your machine</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What Leaks Without Protection</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your real IP. Location revealed.</li>
    <li>Whatever you were sending. Readable.</li>
    <li>Login sessions. Yikes.</li>
    <li>Every site you're visiting</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On <a href="/blog/vpn-public-wifi-guide/">public WiFi</a>, this happens in front of everyone. Including anyone
    specifically hunting on that
    network. It happens.
  </p>

  <!-- Types of Kill Switches -->
  <h2 id="types-of-kill-switches">Three Types of Kill Switches</h2>

  <h3>App-Level</h3>
  <p>
    Blocks only specific apps you choose. Browser keeps working, everything else too. Problem? Anything
    you forgot to add still leaks.
  </p>

  <h3>System-Level</h3>
  <p>
    Blocks everything. Complete protection. All apps lose internet when VPN drops. This is what I use.
  </p>

  <h3>Permanent</h3>
  <p>
    Blocks ALL non-VPN traffic, always. Even when VPN app isn't running. Can't accidentally browse
    unprotected. Very strict though. Can lock you out.
  </p>

  <h3>My Take</h3>
  <p>
    System-level for most people. Complete protection without being annoying.
  </p>

  <!-- VPNs with Best Kill Switches -->
  <h2 id="best-vpns">Best VPNs for Kill Switch</h2>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={killSwitchColumns}
      rows={killSwitchRows}
      caption="VPN kill switch comparison"
  />

  <h3>NordVPN</h3>
  <p>
    Both app-level and system-level options. The system-wide one ("Internet Kill Switch") blocks
    everything. Tested it myself. Works.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/nordvpn"
      text="Get NordVPN . Kill Switch That Works"
      variant="primary"
      program="nordvpn"
  />

  <h3>ExpressVPN</h3>
  <p>
    Calls theirs "Network Lock." System-level, extremely reliable. Caught every simulated drop in
    my testing. Zero leakage.
  </p>
  <p>
    Heads up for 2026: ExpressVPN renamed Network Lock to "Internet Kill Switch." Same feature, new label. It's in the
    Windows, Mac, Linux, router, Android and iOS apps.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/expressvpn"
      text="Get ExpressVPN . Internet Kill Switch"
      variant="primary"
      program="expressvpn"
  />

  <h3>ProtonVPN</h3>
  <p>
    Has a permanent option. Your device literally can't access internet without VPN running. Great
    for <a href="/guides/secure-home-wifi-network/">high-security situations</a>.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/protonvpn"
      text="Get ProtonVPN - Permanent Kill Switch"
      variant="primary"
      program="protonvpn"
  />

  <!-- How to Test Your Kill Switch -->
  <h2 id="setup-by-device">Turn It On (Device by Device)</h2>
  <p>
    Most VPN apps ship with the kill switch off. Annoying, but that's how it is. Here's where to look.
  </p>

  <h3>Windows and Mac</h3>
  <p>
    Inside the VPN app. NordVPN has it under settings as "Kill Switch," with separate toggles for the Internet Kill Switch
    and the App Kill Switch. ExpressVPN puts its Internet Kill Switch in the app options. Flip the system-wide one on and
    forget about it.
  </p>

  <h3>Android</h3>
  <p>
    Android has its own version built in, which is nice. Go to Settings, search "VPN," tap the gear next to your VPN, and
    turn on Always-on VPN. Then turn on "Block connections without VPN." That second toggle is the real kill switch. Works
    on Android 7 and up.
  </p>

  <h3>iPhone and iPad</h3>
  <p>
    No system toggle here. You rely on the app. ExpressVPN and NordVPN both have one for iOS. Read the next section
    before you trust it completely, though.
  </p>

  <h3>Linux</h3>
  <p>
    NordVPN's Linux app is command line. <code>nordvpn set killswitch 1</code> turns it on, <code>0</code> turns it off.
    If your internet dies after you disconnect, that's the kill switch doing its job. Turn it off first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Where Kill Switches Fall Short</h2>
  <p>
    A kill switch catches drops. It doesn't catch everything. Two things worth knowing about.
  </p>

  <h3>TunnelVision</h3>
  <p>
    In May 2024, Leviathan Security published an attack called TunnelVision (CVE-2024-3661). Someone on the same network
    runs a rogue DHCP server and uses option 121 to push routes that pull your traffic outside the VPN tunnel. The VPN
    looks connected the whole time. So the kill switch never fires. Researchers said there isn't a full fix on every
    major OS.
  </p>
  <p>
    Proton says its app is broadly resistant on Android and Windows, and elsewhere once you turn on the right settings. On sketchy public
    WiFi, this is one more reason to use your phone's hotspot instead when it matters.
  </p>

  <h3>iOS Leaks</h3>
  <p>
    iPhones have a long-running problem. Proton reported back in 2020 that iOS keeps some existing connections outside
    the tunnel. Apple added a kill switch option for developers in iOS 14, but Proton found in 2022 that some Apple
    service traffic still leaks. IVPN went as far as removing its iOS kill switch in 2023 because of it. As of 2025,
    security researcher Michael Horowitz was still documenting leaks. The takeaway: on iPhone, a kill switch helps, but
    it's not airtight.
  </p>

  <h3>Split Tunneling Conflicts</h3>
  <p>
    Using split tunneling so some apps skip the VPN? NordVPN has a whole support page on how the two interact,
    because they can conflict. Read it before you combine them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-to-test">Test Yours. Seriously.</h2>
  <p>Don't assume it works. Test it:</p>

  <h3>Quick Test</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Connect VPN, verify your IP changed</li>
    <li>Open terminal, run continuous ping: <code>ping google.com -t</code></li>
    <li>Kill your VPN (simulate a drop)</li>
    <li>Pings should stop immediately</li>
    <li>Reconnect. Pings resume</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>If It Fails</h3>
  <p>
    If your real IP showed up or pings continued? Kill switch isn't working. Check settings. Update the
    app. Or switch VPNs.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Recommendation -->
  <h2 id="recommendation">Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    Had a scare at a coworking space in Lisbon last year. VPN dropped for maybe ten seconds while I was logged
    into a client's admin panel. Didn't notice until later. Might have been fine. Might not have. Kill switch
    would've blocked everything instantly.
  </p>
  <p>
    So yeah. Turn it on. Settings, kill switch, enable. System-level if your VPN offers it. Takes about
    fifteen seconds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your VPN doesn't have a reliable one? That's a reason to switch. <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads/">NordVPN
    and ExpressVPN</a> both get this right.
  </p>

  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/nordvpn"
      text="Get NordVPN . Kill Switch That Works"
      variant="primary"
      program="nordvpn"
  />

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
